% ============================================================================
% clause-type — the two structural kinds of clause and what makes a clause
% one or the other (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Every clause has a subject and a verb, but only SOME clauses can stand
% alone as a complete sentence — that single fact is what splits every
% clause in English into exactly one of two kinds. Recall is a binding
% query:
%
%     ? clause_type(independent_clause, $D)
%       % is_a_clause_that_alone_is_a_complete_sentence
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `clause_type(type, description)` carrying the source citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on a clause-related term that is not one of these
% two structural types. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description
% and recall which type it names:
%
%     ? clause_type($T, is_a_clause_that_alone_is_not_a_complete_sentence)
%       % dependent_clause
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Independent and Dependent
% Clauses: Rules and Examples" article, which states the split as two
% clean, parallel, single-fact sentences:
%
%     type                  description                                          quote (verbatim)
%     --------------------  ---------------------------------------------------  ----------------------------------------------------------------
%     independent_clause    is_a_clause_that_alone_is_a_complete_sentence         "If the clause alone is a complete sentence, it is an independent clause."
%     dependent_clause      is_a_clause_that_alone_is_not_a_complete_sentence     "If the clause alone is not a complete sentence, it is a dependent clause."
%
% WebFetch-verified twice — the second pass specifically pulled the first
% three occurrences of each term from the top of the article, in order, to
% confirm these two sentences (not the surrounding elaboration, which
% bundles in extra facts like "an independent clause ... is a simple
% sentence") are the article's own clean defining pair. `trust consensus`
% — Grammarly is a reputable secondary reference, the same tier the
% sibling `determiner-type.adj`/`noun-type.adj`/`verb-type.adj`/
% `pronoun-type.adj`/`conjunction-type.adj` (the other libraries in this
% directory) already use for their Grammarly citations.
%
% Unlike most sibling tables in this directory, independent/dependent is a
% genuinely EXHAUSTIVE split, not an arbitrary subset of a longer list —
% the article's own opening line states "every clause is either one or
% the other." So honest abstention is instead demonstrated with
% "noun_clause" — a real, well-documented clause category (Grammarly has
% its own dedicated "A Comprehensive Guide to Noun Clauses" article), but
% one that names a FUNCTIONAL role a dependent clause can play, not a
% third structural type alongside these two — so a recall on it ABSTAINS
% rather than inventing a description. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table clause_type {
    columns type, description

    row (independent_clause, is_a_clause_that_alone_is_a_complete_sentence)
    row (dependent_clause, is_a_clause_that_alone_is_not_a_complete_sentence)

    source "If the clause alone is a complete sentence, it is an independent clause."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/grammar/independent-and-dependent-clauses/"
    trust consensus
}
